WEBVTT

00:00.180 --> 00:02.760
Instructor: So this next section is very short,

00:02.760 --> 00:05.220
and as you can see, there's really not a whole lot we cover.

00:05.220 --> 00:08.580
It's all about setting up your own custom Git aliases.

00:08.580 --> 00:10.080
So there's two things we talk about.

00:10.080 --> 00:14.190
One is how we define the aliases and where we put them,

00:14.190 --> 00:16.140
which is this global config file.

00:16.140 --> 00:18.180
That's normally where we put them.

00:18.180 --> 00:21.810
So I'll say, eh, I don't know, it's worth knowing about that

00:21.810 --> 00:24.090
even outside of the context of aliases,

00:24.090 --> 00:27.540
and then actually writing these aliases is nice to have.

00:27.540 --> 00:29.730
Some people really use a lot of aliases.

00:29.730 --> 00:30.750
They're like shortcuts,

00:30.750 --> 00:32.490
little custom commands you can write,

00:32.490 --> 00:35.340
so you don't have to type out full length Git commands,

00:35.340 --> 00:37.230
but other people aren't as crazy about them.

00:37.230 --> 00:39.300
So I'd say completely nice to have,

00:39.300 --> 00:41.040
but people might argue with me.

00:41.040 --> 00:43.770
Some people are very, I don't wanna say addicted,

00:43.770 --> 00:48.450
but very into their own aliases that they've set up,

00:48.450 --> 00:49.890
so much so that they might not even know

00:49.890 --> 00:51.570
how to use regular Git commands.

00:51.570 --> 00:53.100
They're just so used to,

00:53.100 --> 00:55.140
so accustomed to their custom aliases.

00:55.140 --> 00:56.640
Anyway, nothing critical.

00:56.640 --> 00:58.710
It's a short section and pretty light stuff.

00:58.710 --> 01:00.030
It's kind of fun.

01:00.030 --> 01:02.310
And if you enjoy working with these aliases,

01:02.310 --> 01:04.960
you'll have a lot that you can do after this section.
